  "updated": "2026-08-16T15:38:33.574733+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nShowers and storms from this evening will continue to die off\novernight as the shortwave pushes through the region. This will\nmean that the first part of Sunday will be dry for nearly the\nentire forecast area. What is a possibility for many areas though\nis fog. With widespread dewpoints in the 50s due to the easterly\nwinds, there is plenty of moisture present and there is likely\neven higher values in localized area that saw precipitation this\nevening. \n\nLight easterly winds will aid in upslope flow across the region\nwith even further enhancement along the east facing mountain\nslopes. As we get into an area of broad subsidence behind the\naforementioned shortwave, most of the clouds should clear out. If\nthis does end up being the scenario, then widespread fog is \nanticipated. Locally dense fog is possible as well, especially in\nriver valleys, areas of recent rainfall, and locations near the \neast slopes of mountains. Confidence is further increased in this \nscenario playing out as the same regime / ingredients were \npresent Saturday morning when locally dense fog was observed in \nseveral locations. \n\nLater in the day today there will be another vort max that moves\nthrough Montana aided by a weakly digging trough over the Canadian\nRockies. This feature will bring yet another round of showers and\nthunderstorms as it quickly pushes through the region. There is\npotential for a strong to isolated severe storm in far SE Montana\nearly Sunday Evening as the area of lift moves into a region with\nmodest CAPE and higher PWATs. The shear is not overly impressive\nbut there is modest mid-level lapse rates of 7-8 C/Km which could\nsustain updrafts in conjunction with 20kts of speed sheer SFC -\n3km AGL. In short, widespread showers and storms are expected with\nthis vort max, and I would not be surprised to see a stronger\nstorm in Carter Co. MT this evening. \n\nMonday into Tuesday there is decent agreement amongst models that\na shortwave will push through NE MT with a trailing cold front\nthrough our area. While this front will not bring in any\nmeaningful cooldown this will be another shot at precipitation for\nmost of the CWA. The timing varies between Monday evening and \nTuesday midday for when exactly the front will move through as \nwell as exact precip amounts. The NBM 10-90th shows a spread of \n0-0.4 for most of the CWA and due to the showery nature of the \nprecipitation, it will likely be yet another tale of those who see\na lot of precip and those who see none.\n\nGood news for those who love summer, ensembles are consistently\nshowing a rige building back into the intermountain region \nmidweek. These same ensembles do disagree on temperature values \nthough. Some show highs slightly above average for the middle to \nlate part of next week while others show us flirting with triple \ndigits again next weekend. This will likely depend on the \ndirection of our surface winds and whether or not this ridge \ninduces any monsoonal moisture / storms. WMR",
